course: books
lesson: 2
title: Alice in Wonderland, chapter I, part 2: The hall of doors, DRINK ME and EAT ME
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Alice jumps up after her fall, sees the White Rabbit hurrying down a passage and races after him, but he turns a corner and vanishes, leaving her in a long, low hall lit by lamps.
explain: All the doors are locked; then she finds a little glass table with a tiny golden key, which opens only a little door fifteen inches high, and through it she sees the loveliest garden, but she is far too big to get in.
explain: Back at the table a bottle labelled "DRINK ME" has appeared; sensible Alice checks it is not marked "poison", tastes it, finds it delicious and shrinks to ten inches.
explain: But she has left the key on top of the glass table, cannot climb the slippery leg, and sits down and cries, then tells herself off sharply.
explain: Under the table she finds a little cake marked "EAT ME", reasons that any change of size will help, and eats it all: the part ends on a cliffhanger, full of hope and worry at once.
fact: The Rabbit, turning a corner, says "Oh my ears and whiskers, how late it's getting!", and then disappears.
fact: The hall is long and low, lit by a row of lamps hanging from the roof, with locked doors all round.
fact: On a three-legged table of solid glass Alice finds only a tiny golden key; it is too small for the big doors.
fact: On her second time round the hall she finds a low curtain, and behind it a little door about fifteen inches high, which the key fits.
fact: The door leads into a passage "not much larger than a rat-hole", and beyond it lies "the loveliest garden you ever saw", with bright flowers and cool fountains, but Alice "could not even get her head through the doorway".
fact: Alice wishes she could "shut up like a telescope", meaning fold down smaller like a pull-out telescope.
fact: So many out-of-the-way things had happened that Alice "had begun to think that very few things indeed were really impossible".
fact: A little bottle labelled "DRINK ME" appears on the table, "which certainly was not here before", Alice says.
fact: Alice remembers stories of children who were burnt or eaten by wild beasts because they forgot simple rules, so she checks the bottle is not marked "poison"; one rule she remembers is that if you drink much from a bottle marked "poison", "it is almost certain to disagree with you" (make you ill).
fact: The drink tastes of cherry-tart, custard, pine-apple, roast turkey, toffee and hot buttered toast, and it shrinks her to "only ten inches high".
fact: She waits to see if she will shrink further, worried she might go out "like a candle".
fact: She forgot the key on the table; the glass leg is too slippery to climb, so she cries.
fact: Alice "generally gave herself very good advice, (though she very seldom followed it)", and she once tried to box her own ears for cheating at croquet against herself; to "box someone's ears" means to slap them on the side of the head, which nobody should do today.
fact: "This curious child was very fond of pretending to be two people", but now there was "hardly enough of me left to make one respectable person".
fact: A glass box under the table holds a small cake with "EAT ME" marked in currants (small dried grapes); her plan: "if it makes me grow larger, I can reach the key; and if it makes me grow smaller, I can creep under the door", so either way she gets into the garden.
fact: After the first little bite nothing happened and she stayed the same size; this seemed "quite dull and stupid", because she now expected only out-of-the-way things, so she ate the whole cake.
fact: Old words in today's English: "alas" = sadly, "ventured" = dared, "set to work" = got busy, "leave off" = stop, "curious" = strange (and also wanting to know).
exercise: What was the first thing Alice saw after she jumped up from the leaves? | Another long passage, with the Rabbit hurrying down it | A bright garden full of flowers and fountains | Her sister sitting on the bank far above her | A glass table holding a small golden key
exercise: What did the Rabbit say as it turned the corner? | "Oh my ears and whiskers, how late it's getting!" | "Oh dear! Oh dear! I shall be late!" | "Run home this moment and fetch me my gloves and my fan!" | "Who are you, and what do you want here?"
exercise: How was the long, low hall lit? | By a row of lamps hanging from the roof | By sunshine through a hole in the ceiling | By candles burning on every single door | By a big fire roaring at one end of the hall
exercise: What was wrong with all the doors round the hall? | They were all locked | They were all painted shut | They were all far too small | They all led back up the well
exercise: How many legs did the glass table have? | Three | Four | Two | Seven
exercise: What lay on the glass table the first time? | Only a tiny golden key | A bottle and a cake | A map and a picture | A watch and a pair of gloves
exercise: Why could the key not open the big doors? | The locks were too large, or the key too small | The key was made of chocolate and melted | The big doors had no keyholes in them at all | Alice could not reach up as high as the locks
exercise: Where was the little door hidden? | Behind a low curtain | Under the glass table | Inside a cupboard in the well | At the bottom of a pool
exercise: About how high was the little door? | Fifteen inches | Ten inches | Four feet | Twenty-five inches
exercise: What did Alice see through the little door? | The loveliest garden, with flowers and fountains | A dark kitchen full of smoke and pepper | The riverbank where her sister was sitting | A tiny room with a bed and a white rabbit in it
exercise: Why could Alice not get into the garden at first? | She could not even get her head through the doorway | The door slammed shut as soon as she looked in | A big dog was guarding the garden from inside | The Rabbit had locked it again with another key
exercise: Why did Alice think very few things were impossible now? | So many out-of-the-way things had happened lately | Her sister had told her magic was always real | She had read it in her book of rules for telescopes | The Rabbit had promised to teach her some magic
exercise: What new thing was on the table the second time? | A little bottle | A golden key | A jar of orange jam | A glass box
exercise: What was written on the bottle's label? | "DRINK ME" | "EAT ME" | "POISON" | "ORANGE MARMALADE"
exercise: Before drinking, what did Alice look for on the bottle? | The word "poison" | The Rabbit's name | A price ticket | The date it was made
exercise: Which of these was NOT one of the flavours of the drink? | Strawberry jam | Custard | Hot buttered toast | Roast turkey
exercise: How tall was Alice after drinking? | Ten inches | Two inches | Fifteen inches | Two feet
exercise: What did Alice fear might happen if she kept shrinking? | She might go out altogether, like a candle | She might turn into a mouse | She might fall back up the hole | She might never see Dinah's milk
exercise: Why could Alice not get the key back? | The glass leg was too slippery to climb | The Rabbit had taken the key away with him | The key had shrunk too small to be seen | The table had vanished into thin air
exercise: What did Alice do when she could not reach the key? | Sat down and cried | Called the Rabbit for help | Tried to break the glass | Went to sleep under the table
exercise: What does the book say about Alice's good advice to herself? | She very seldom followed it | She always followed it at once | She only gave it to her sister | She wrote it down in a book
exercise: Where did Alice find the cake? | In a little glass box under the table | On top of the glass table next to the key | Behind the low curtain by the little door | In a cupboard on the side of the deep well
exercise: What spelled "EAT ME" on the cake? | Currants | Icing | Cherries | Nuts
exercise: After one little bite, what happened to Alice's size? | Nothing; she stayed the same size | She shot up to the ceiling | She shrank to five inches | She grew only in her neck
exercise: Here "ventured" means... | Dared | Refused | Forgot | Hurried
exercise: Thinking back to the fall: what had Alice put in a cupboard on the way down? | An empty marmalade jar | The tiny golden key | The bottle marked "DRINK ME" | Her sister's book with no pictures
exercise: Thinking back to the start: what did the Rabbit worry that it would be? | Late | Lost | Hungry | Asleep
test: Ollie goes: "Alice just glugged the bottle straight away, she's well reckless." Put him right. | She first checked it was not marked "poison" | She asked the Rabbit if it was safe to drink it | She made Dinah taste a drop of it first of all | She only smelled it and never drank a drop at all
test: Zara, Gen Z: "The garden was giving main character energy, but Alice couldn't even fit, bruh." ("giving main character energy" = looking amazing and special.) Why couldn't she fit at first? | She was much too big for the doorway | The garden gate was locked from inside | She was too scared to crawl through | The Rabbit was blocking the way
test: What would have happened if Alice had picked up the key before she drank? | She could have unlocked the little door and gone in | The key would have grown too heavy for her to carry | The bottle would have refused to make her any smaller | The little door would have vanished behind the curtain
test: Jake from Yorkshire: "She were right mardy, crying on t' floor." ("mardy" = grumpy or sulky.) What does Alice do after crying? | She tells herself off sharply to stop | She falls asleep under the glass table | She shouts for her big sister to help | She goes back up the passage to the well
test: True or false: Alice thinks it is dull that the first bite of cake does nothing. | True: she expected only out-of-the-way things now | False: she is happy to stay the same size | True: she wanted to grow taller than the Rabbit | False: she never notices her size at all
test: Why does the book call it "stupid" that life went "on in the common way" after the cake? | Alice now expected strange things to happen every time | The cake tasted stale and dry and quite horrible | Her sister had said that cake makes you stupid | Alice was tired and wanted to go back to bed
test: Dad, sarcastic: "Oh brilliant, shrink yourself and leave the key on the table, genius." What mistake does he mean? | Alice forgot the key before she shrank | Alice drank a bottle of poison by accident | Alice lost the key down the well | Alice gave the key to the Rabbit
test: Why does Alice say there is "hardly enough of me left to make one respectable person"? | She is so tiny now, and she likes pretending to be two | She has lost both her shoes and her stockings | She is hungry and has not eaten all day long | The Rabbit said she was not respectable enough
test: The book tells of children who were burnt or eaten by wild beasts because they forgot simple rules. What is it saying about Alice? | She remembers the rules, so she checks the bottle | She had been eaten by a wild beast before | She never listens to any rules at all | She learnt the rules from the White Rabbit
test: Gran in Cornwall: "That cake were proper job, my lover." ("proper job" = really good, "my lover" = a friendly way to say dear.) What is the cake's message written in? | Currants | Icing sugar | Chocolate | Jam
test: Looking back over the whole book so far: what has Alice followed, from the bank to the hall? | The White Rabbit | Her cat Dinah | Her big sister | A trail of currants
test: Looking back: when Alice fell, she thought she would come out among the "Antipathies". Now at the bottom, where is she really? | In a long hall with locked doors | In New Zealand or else in Australia | At the centre of the earth | Back on the riverbank
test: Compare Alice now with Alice at the start, when she found her sister's book dull. What has changed? | She is busy, curious and puzzling things out | She is even more bored than she was before | She has fallen asleep and stopped thinking | She has gone home and found a better book
test: ★★★ The "EAT ME" plan: "if it makes me grow larger, I can reach the key; and if it makes me grow smaller, I can creep under the door". Which result would NOT get her into the garden? | Staying the same size | Growing larger | Growing smaller | Growing very small
test: ★★★ The little door was about fifteen inches high, and the drink made Alice ten inches high. How much shorter than the door was she? | Five inches | Ten inches | Fifteen inches | Twenty-five inches
test: ★★★ Read carefully: did Alice find the little door the first time she went round the hall? | No, on the second time round | Yes, at once, behind the table | Yes, because the Rabbit showed her | No, only on the third time round
exam: Identify the object discovered by the protagonist upon the three-legged table of solid glass on the first occasion. | A diminutive golden key | A bottle bearing a label | A cake marked in currants | A box constructed of glass
exam: Ascertain the precaution observed by the protagonist prior to the consumption of the contents of the bottle. | She examined it for the word "poison" | She requested the Rabbit's permission to drink | She first poured a quantity upon the floor | She awaited the advice of her elder sister
exam: It is given that the protagonist attained a height of ten inches. Determine the principal obstacle which subsequently prevented her entry into the garden. | The key had been left upon the table beyond her reach | The door had been locked anew by the White Rabbit | The curtain had been drawn across the doorway | Her stature remained excessive for the passage
exam: Select the response that most accurately characterises the protagonist's habit as regards counsel addressed to herself. | It was generally sound but seldom followed | It was invariably followed without delay | It was given exclusively to her elder sister | It was offered solely in the French language
